<p>Safari is the closest Orion Browser alternative for an Apple-focused workflow. Firefox suits readers who want a different desktop engine and Firefox extensions, while Brave combines Chromium compatibility with built-in blocking. Choose Vivaldi for detailed tab organization, DuckDuckGo for simpler privacy controls, or Chrome when a required work extension drives the decision. None reproduces Orion's unusual mix of WebKit and Chrome/Firefox extension support on every device. There is also a recent platform change: Kagi has stopped its own Linux and Windows development. Pick by your devices and essential extensions before moving bookmarks.</p>
<h2>What changed, and when keeping Orion makes sense</h2>
<p><a href="https://orionbrowser.com/">Orion</a> is Kagi's browser, rather than the Kagi search subscription. Its attraction is a native Apple experience with built-in blocking and support for extensions originally written for other browsers. That combination can be more valuable than a long list of generic browser features.</p>
<p>However, Kagi's <a href="https://blog.kagi.com/update-orion-linux-windows">October 2, 2026 announcement</a> says it is ending direct development for Linux and Windows and opening those projects for community continuation. Development is concentrating on macOS and iOS. The <a href="https://orionbrowser.com/platforms/linux">Linux page</a> still offers a beta, but now carries the development-ending notice. An available download should not be mistaken for a promise of continued Kagi maintenance.</p>
<p>On Apple devices, keep Orion if the particular extensions you need work reliably and you like its interface. Kagi describes <a href="https://help.kagi.com/orion/browser-extensions/macos-extensions.html">macOS extension support</a> as incomplete and still in beta. One unsupported API can prevent an extension from working. Its <a href="https://help.kagi.com/orion/browser-extensions/ios-ipados-extensions.html">iOS and iPadOS documentation</a> explains that mobile support has additional limitations. This is a concrete reason to trial a replacement when a password manager or work extension fails, rather than proof that Orion is unsuitable for everyone.</p>
<p>Cost alone is a weak switching argument. The browser is free. <a href="https://orionbrowser.com/orionplus">Orion+</a> is optional support, advertised at US$5 monthly, US$50 yearly, or US$150 for lifetime access, plus sales tax. Subscriptions renew and some add-ons may cost extra. A Kagi Search subscription is not required for Orion+.</p>
<h2>Compare the replacement you actually need</h2>
<p>This is a research-based comparison of official product pages and documentation, checked October 8, 2026. We did not benchmark battery life, measure tracker blocking, or run these browsers through an extension test suite. Recommendations below are editorial judgments about workflow fit.</p>
<p>All six offer free core browsing. That does not make paid extensions, VPNs, AI services, or third-party subscriptions free.</p>
